The '''Silkie''', also known as the '''Silky''' or '''Chinese silk chicken''', is a Chinese [[List of chicken breeds|breed]] of [[chicken]]s named for its atypically fluffy [[plumage]], which is soft to the touch, like silk or fur. Its other unusual qualities include black skin and [[bone]]s, blue [[earlobe]]s, and [[pentadactyly]] (five toes on each foot) – most chickens have only four. 

The birds are commonly reared for exhibition in [[poultry show]]s, and a number of [[list of chicken colours|color varieties]] have been bred; the [[Entente Européenne]] recognises 10 plumage colors, the [[American Poultry Association]] seven, and the [[Poultry Club of Great Britain]] five.{{r|ee|apa|pcgb}} Both bearded and non-bearded forms of most colours exist. In European countries, both [[bantam (poultry)|bantam]] and large fowl are recognised, while in the United States, only the bantam is listed.{{r|ee|apa}}

Silkie hens show a strong tendency to [[broodiness]] and are commonly used to hatch eggs from other breeds and bird species; as a result of the same tendency, they lay relatively few eggs.
